    {
      "id": "j202-lambdas-jshell",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: jshell-mode-lambdas-as-first-class-f\n//@ description: 🔵 JShell Mode — lambdas as first-class functions\n// --- JShell mode: lambdas, and the interfaces that give them a type ---\nimport java.util.function.*;\n\n// A lambda has no type of its own in Java. It takes the type of the functional\n// interface it is assigned to - an interface with exactly one abstract method.\n// Function<String,Integer> means \"takes a String, returns an Integer\", and the\n// single method to implement is apply().\nFunction<String, Integer> length = s -> s.length();\n\n// Predicate<T> is the special case \"returns boolean\"; its method is test().\nPredicate<Integer>  isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;\n\n// Consumer<T> takes a value and returns nothing; its method is accept().\n// System.out::println is a method reference: the same thing as\n// s -> System.out.println(s), but without naming a parameter you never use.\nConsumer<String>    print  = System.out::println;\n\n// Supplier<T> is the mirror image: no input, produces a value via get().\n// Nothing is evaluated here - the clock is only read when get() is called.\nSupplier<String>    now    = () -> java.time.LocalTime.now().toString();\n\nSystem.out.println(length.apply(\"Hello\"));\nSystem.out.println(isEven.test(42));\nprint.accept(\"Method reference!\");\nSystem.out.println(\"Time: \" + now.get());\n\n// BiFunction<A,B,R>: two inputs, one result. Math::max is a reference to a\n// static method whose shape (int, int) -> int already matches.\nBiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> max = Math::max;\nSystem.out.println(\"max(3,7) = \" + max.apply(3, 7));\n\n// andThen composes left to right: run 'length' first, feed its result to the\n// next stage. (compose() would be the other order.) String::toUpperCase works\n// as a Function<String,String> because the receiver becomes the argument.\nvar shout = length.andThen(n -> n + \" chars\").andThen(String::toUpperCase);\nSystem.out.println(shout.apply(\"hello\"));",
      "output": "",
      "returnValue": null,
      "executed": false,
      "executionCount": null,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "jshell-mode-lambdas-as-first-class-f",
